\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Mortuary Practices in the Upper Valley: A Preliminary Report}
\author{H. Osei and L. Marchetti}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{intro}
The upper valley survey recorded forty-two burial contexts across six
sites. As Section~\ref{conclusion} argues, the sample is small but
instructive.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
Earlier reports treated the valley as a single cultural horizon.
Recent compositional studies of grave goods suggest at least two
distinct traditions.

\section{Field Methods}
\label{methods}
Each context was excavated in ten-centimetre spits and recorded
photographically; Section~\ref{sec-results} gives the resulting counts.
Soil samples were floated for botanical remains on site.

\section{Results}
\label{sec-results}
Thirty-one contexts contained ceramic offerings, and eleven contained
worked bone. Counts follow the conventions described in
Section~\ref{methods}.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{conclusion}
The valley shows a two-phase mortuary sequence rather than one
long-lived tradition. Further radiocarbon work is planned for the
coming season.
\end{document}

